.TH test 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
test, [ \- evaluate a condition
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B test
.I expression
.br
.BI [ " expression" " ]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B test
evaluates
.I expression
and exits 0 if it is true, 1 if it is false, and 2 if the expression
itself was wrong.  It is what
.B if
and
.B while
in a shell script are built on.
.PP
.B [
is the same program under another name; when called that way the last
argument must be
.BR ] .
.SH FILE TESTS
.TP
.BI \-e " file"
Exists.
.TP
.BI \-f " file"
Exists and is an ordinary file.
.TP
.BI \-d " file"
Exists and is a directory.
.TP
.BI \-b " file"
Exists and is a block device.
.TP
.BI \-c " file"
Exists and is a character device.
.TP
.BI \-p " file"
Exists and is a FIFO.
.TP
.BI \-r " file"
Readable.
.TP
.BI \-w " file"
Writable.
.TP
.BI \-x " file"
Executable.
.TP
.BI \-s " file"
Exists and is not empty.
.TP
.BI \-u " file"
Has the set-uid bit set.
.TP
.BI \-g " file"
Has the set-gid bit set.
.TP
.IB file1 " \-ef " file2
The same file: same device and inode.
.TP
.IB file1 " \-nt " file2
.I file1
is newer.
.TP
.IB file1 " \-ot " file2
.I file1
is older.
.SH STRING TESTS
.TP
.BI \-z " string"
Empty.
.TP
.BI \-n " string"
Not empty.
.TP
.IB s1 " = " s2
Equal.
.TP
.IB s1 " != " s2
Not equal.
.PP
A bare
.I string
is true when it is not empty, which is the usual way to test that a
variable is set.
.SH NUMBER TESTS
.IB n1 " \-eq " n2
.br
.IB n1 " \-ne " n2
.br
.IB n1 " \-lt " n2
.br
.IB n1 " \-le " n2
.br
.IB n1 " \-gt " n2
.br
.IB n1 " \-ge " n2
.SH OTHER
.TP
.BI \-t " fd"
That file descriptor is a terminal.
.SH COMBINING
.B !
negates,
.B \-a
is and,
.B \-o
is or, and parentheses group \- though the shell needs them quoted, as
.B \(
and
.BR \) .
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
if test -f /etc/wifi.conf
then
	wifi -f
fi

while [ -n "$1" ]
do
	echo $1
	shift
done
.fi
.SH NOTES
This is the Mark Williams
.BR test ,
which is a good deal more complete than the V7 one: the file age tests
.RB ( \-nt ,
.BR \-ot ,
.BR \-ef )
and
.B \-e
are all here.
.PP
The shell has no
.B test
built in, so every
.B if
in a script is a fork and an exec.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sh (1),
.BR expr (1)
